🌐 中文版：繁體中文
Problem statement The problem gives an integer array nums that is sorted, and we want to find the index of the element target in it, returning -1 if it isn’t found. It also requires that the algorithm run in $O(\log n)$ time. SolutionMy approachint search(int* nums, int numsSize, int target) &#123; // init int i,mid=0,low=0, high=numsSize-1; // Binary search // 1. find lower index and high index according to the length of array // 2 calculate middle value // 3. update low and high value // 4. again calcuate the new middle value until derive the target value or return -1 while(low &lt;= high)&#123; // needs to consider the situation that the middle number is not an even number mid = (int)((low + high)/2.0); // need to find the lower section of array if(nums[mid] &gt; target)&#123; high = mid-1; &#125; else if(nums[mid] &lt; target)&#123; low = mid+1; &#125; else &#123; // find value return mid; &#125; &#125; return -1;&#125;
This uses the standard binary search approach. First we set the middle value mid where we split, taking the average of low and high to find the midpoint. Then we start judging the cases:
When nums[mid] &gt; target: the midpoint is greater than the target, meaning we can discard the right half of mid and search only the left half, so we adjust high to shrink the search range high = mid -1;
When nums[mid] &lt; target: the midpoint is less than the target, meaning we can discard the left half of mid and search only the right half, so we adjust low to shrink the search range low = mid +1; The third case is finding the target value in the array, so we return mid The while loop’s termination condition is low &gt; high, meaning we’ve searched too many times and confirmed the array has no target value, so we end the loop and return -1
Note: for the midpoint it’s better to rewrite it as mid = low + (high - low)/2. Doing so avoids the overflow problem when both high and low are large numbers and their sum overflows.
Execution result Another approach: finding the upper bound This is another approach I saw in the LeetCode solutions section. Rather than finding the Target itself directly, find the position where Target could be inserted into the array. This solution first defines the block that can be inserted into, as the picture describes: for an array like [-7,-4,3,9,9,9,12], the largest position it could be inserted at is between 9 and 12, and the smallest position it could be inserted at is between 3 and 9. First, same as before, we need to compute the midpoint position, then when judging we consider separately:
When nums[mid] &lt; target: the midpoint is less than the target, so the possible insert position is to the right of mid, so we adjust the low range and keep looking for the upper bound low = mid +1; When nums[mid] == target: the midpoint equals the target, so the possible insert position is to the right of mid, so we adjust the low range and keep looking for the upper bound low = mid +1; When nums[mid] &gt; target: the midpoint is greater than the target, so the possible insert position is to the left of mid, so we adjust the high range and keep looking for the upper bound. Here mid might also be an insertable position, so we set high to mid high = mid;
Once the loop ends, high represents the insert position, and low - 1 represents the largest element not greater than target. So after it ends we need to check whether nums[low-1] equals target; if so return low-1, otherwise return -1. The algorithm is as follows: int search(int* nums, int numsSize, int target) &#123; // array init int i,mid=0,low=0, high=numsSize-1; // Approach-2 Find Upper Bound while (low &lt; high)&#123; mid = low + (int)((high-low)/2.0); if (nums[mid] &lt;= target)&#123; low = mid +1; &#125; else &#123; high = mid; &#125; &#125; if( low &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; target == nums[low-1])&#123; return low -1; &#125; else if( nums[low] == target &amp;&amp; low ==0 )&#123; // edge case return low; &#125; else if ( high == numsSize-1 &amp;&amp; target == nums[high])&#123; //edge case return high; &#125; else &#123; return -1; &#125;&#125;
Why does this method find the target? Merging conditions: in this method we merge the two conditions nums[mid] &lt; target and nums[mid] == target, because whether it’s less than or equal, the target’s insert position should be to the right of mid. So in both cases we set left to mid + 1. Keeping the valid range: if nums[mid] &gt; target, the target should be at mid or to its left, so we set right to mid rather than mid - 1, keeping mid as a possible position. Loop termination check: when left and right are equal, the loop ends, and left is the target’s insert position. If the target exists in the array, then left - 1 is the last position of the target. At that point we just need to check whether nums[left - 1] equals the target.
Special cases All elements in the array are greater than the target: in this case left ends up equal to 0, which means the target doesn’t exist in the array. All elements in the array are less than the target: in this case left ends up equal to the array length, and the target should be inserted at the last position of the array.
Execution result Time complexityThe number of steps N -&gt; 1&#x2F;2 N -&gt; 1&#x2F;4 N -&gt; 1&#x2F;8 N … converges to a constant following the $\log n$ function.

