The main goal here is to try building a network-layer socket with Winsock on Windows and then do application-layer protocol parsing on top of it. The language is C, the platform is VSCode, and this time I learned by way of the recently very popular ChatGPT, then did my own debugging and dug into the syntax from there.
Sample code#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;#include &lt;string.h&gt;#include &lt;winsock2.h&gt;#define PORT 80 // HTTP uses port 80 by default#define BACKLOG 10 // max length of the pending-connection queue#define BUFFER_SIZE 4096 // size of the receive bufferint main(void)&#123;    // Initialize the WinSock library    WSADATA wsaData;    int result = WSAStartup(MAKEWORD(2, 2), &amp;wsaData);    if (result != 0) &#123;        printf(&quot;WSAStartup failed: %d\n&quot;, result);        return 1;    &#125;    // Create a socket    SOCKET listenSocket = socket(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM, IPPROTO_TCP);    if (listenSocket == INVALID_SOCKET) &#123;        printf(&quot;socket failed: %d\n&quot;, WSAGetLastError());        WSACleanup();        return 1;    &#125;    // Bind the socket to the local address and port    struct sockaddr_in serverAddress;    serverAddress.sin_family = AF_INET;    serverAddress.sin_addr.s_addr = INADDR_ANY;    serverAddress.sin_port = htons(PORT);    result = bind(listenSocket, (SOCKADDR*)&amp;serverAddress, sizeof(serverAddress));    if (result == SOCKET_ERROR) &#123;        printf(&quot;bind failed: %d\n&quot;, WSAGetLastError());        closesocket(listenSocket);        WSACleanup();        return 1;    &#125;    // Listen on the socket    result = listen(listenSocket, BACKLOG);    if (result == SOCKET_ERROR) &#123;        printf(&quot;listen failed: %d\n&quot;, WSAGetLastError());        closesocket(listenSocket);        WSACleanup();        return 1;    &#125;    // Block and wait for a client to connect    printf(&quot;Waiting for client to connect...\n&quot;);    struct sockaddr_in clientAddress;    int clientAddressLen = sizeof(clientAddress);    SOCKET clientSocket = accept(listenSocket, (SOCKADDR*)&amp;clientAddress, &amp;clientAddressLen);    if (clientSocket == INVALID_SOCKET) &#123;        printf(&quot;accept failed: %d\n&quot;, WSAGetLastError());        closesocket(listenSocket);        WSACleanup();        return 1;    &#125;    printf(&quot;Client connected.\n&quot;);        // Read the request the client sent    char buffer[BUFFER_SIZE];    int recvLen = recv(clientSocket, buffer, BUFFER_SIZE, 0);    if (recvLen &lt;= 0) &#123;        printf(&quot;recv failed: %d\n&quot;, WSAGetLastError());        closesocket(clientSocket);        closesocket(listenSocket);        WSACleanup();        return 1;    &#125;    buffer[recvLen] = &#x27;\0&#x27;;    printf(&quot;Received request:\n%s&quot;, buffer);    // Parse the HTTP request    char method[16];    char url[256];    char version[16];    sscanf_s(buffer, &quot;%s %s %s&quot;, method, (unsigned int)sizeof(method), url, (unsigned int)sizeof(url), version, (unsigned int)sizeof(version));    printf(&quot;Parsed request: method=%s, url=%s, version=%s\n&quot;, method, url, version);    // Build the HTTP response    char response[BUFFER_SIZE];    sprintf_s(response, BUFFER_SIZE, &quot;HTTP/1.1 200 OK\r\nContent-Type: text/plain\r\n\r\nHello, World!&quot;);    int responseLen = (int)strlen(response);        // Send the HTTP response    result = send(clientSocket, response, responseLen, 0);    if (result == SOCKET_ERROR) &#123;        printf(&quot;send failed: %d\n&quot;, WSAGetLastError());        closesocket(clientSocket);        closesocket(listenSocket);        WSACleanup();        return 1;    &#125;    printf(&quot;Sent response:\n%s&quot;, response);    // Close the sockets    closesocket(clientSocket);    closesocket(listenSocket);    WSACleanup();    return 0;&#125;

Main program flow
Initialize the WinSock library
Listen on a socket and wait for a client to connect
Receive the client request and parse its method, URL, and protocol version
Build the HTTP response and send it to the client
Close the socket and clean up the Winsock resources

Walking through the code
First you must include the &lt;winsock2.h&gt; header
Then load the WinSock DLL  WSADATA wsaData;int result = WSAStartup(MAKEWORD(2, 2), &amp;wsaData);if (result != 0) &#123;    // error handling&#125;
Create a socket  // Create a socketSOCKET listenSocket = socket(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM, IPPROTO_TCP);if (listenSocket == INVALID_SOCKET) &#123;    // error handling&#125;
Bind the socket to the local address and port  // Bind the socket to the local address and portstruct sockaddr_in serverAddress;serverAddress.sin_family = AF_INET;serverAddress.sin_addr.s_addr = INADDR_ANY;serverAddress.sin_port = htons(PORT);result = bind(listenSocket, (SOCKADDR*)&amp;serverAddress, sizeof(serverAddress));if (result == SOCKET_ERROR) &#123;    // error handling&#125;
Listen on the socket  result = listen(listenSocket, BACKLOG);if (result == SOCKET_ERROR) &#123;    // error handling&#125;
Wait for a client to connect  printf(&quot;Waiting for client to connect...\n&quot;);struct sockaddr_in clientAddress;int clientAddressLen = sizeof(clientAddress);SOCKET clientSocket = accept(listenSocket, (SOCKADDR*)&amp;clientAddress, &amp;clientAddressLen);if (clientSocket == INVALID_SOCKET) &#123;  // error handling&#125;printf(&quot;Client connected.\n&quot;);
Read the client request  char buffer[BUFFER_SIZE];int recvLen = recv(clientSocket, buffer, BUFFER_SIZE, 0);if (recvLen &lt;= 0) &#123;   // error handling&#125;buffer[recvLen] = &#x27;\0&#x27;;printf(&quot;Received request:\n%s&quot;, buffer);
Parse the HTTP request  char method[16];char url[256];char version[16];sscanf_s(buffer, &quot;%s %s %s&quot;, method, (unsigned int)sizeof(method), url, (unsigned int)sizeof(url), version, (unsigned int)sizeof(version));printf(&quot;Parsed request: method=%s, url=%s, version=%s\n&quot;, method, url, version);
Build the HTTP response  char response[BUFFER_SIZE];sprintf_s(response, BUFFER_SIZE, &quot;HTTP/1.1 200 OK\r\nContent-Type: text/plain\r\n\r\nHello, World!&quot;);int responseLen = (int)strlen(response);
Send the HTTP response  result = send(clientSocket, response, responseLen, 0);if (result == SOCKET_ERROR) &#123;    // error handling&#125;printf(&quot;Sent response:\n%s&quot;, response);
Close the sockets  closesocket(clientSocket);closesocket(listenSocket);WSACleanup();

Program output
Start the HTTP server
Visit http://localhost/
Terminal output

Usage and meaning of the functions in Winsock2.h
WSAStartup
Used to initialize the Winsock library. It takes a version argument of type WORD and a struct pointer of type WSADATA
Returns an integer: 0 if the call succeeds, otherwise an error code


socket
Used to create a new socket. It takes three arguments: protocol family, socket type, and protocol type
SOCKET WSAAPI socket(
    [in] int af,
    [in] int type,
    [in] int protocol
);
    - It returns a **SOCKET**- **bind**    - Binds a socket to the local address and port. It takes three arguments: socket descriptor, address, and address length    - ```c=      int WSAAPI bind(       [in] SOCKET         s,       [in] const sockaddr *name,       [in] int            namelen      );


Returns an integer


listen
Used to listen on a socket and wait for a client to connect. It takes two arguments: the socket descriptor and the max length of the pending-connection queue
int WSAAPI listen(
    [in] SOCKET s,
    [in] int    backlog
  );
     - Returns an integer- **accept**    - Accepts a client connection and returns a new socket descriptor    - Takes two arguments: the socket descriptor and a pointer to the client address    - ```c=      SOCKET WSAAPI accept(         [in]      SOCKET   s,         [out]     sockaddr *addr,         [in, out] int      *addrlen      );


Returns a value of type SOCKET representing the new socket descriptor


recv
Receives data sent by the client
Takes four arguments: the socket descriptor, a pointer to the receive buffer, the buffer size, and receive options
int WSAAPI recv(
   [in]  SOCKET s,
   [out] char   *buf,
   [in]  int    len,
   [in]  int    flags
 );
    - Returns an integer- **send**    -  Sends data to the client    -  Takes four arguments: the socket descriptor, a pointer to the send buffer, the buffer size, and send options    -  ```c=       int WSAAPI send(         [in] SOCKET     s,         [in] const char *buf,         [in] int        len,         [in] int        flags        );


Returns an integer


closesocket
Closes a socket
Takes a socket descriptor as its argument
int WSAAPI closesocket(
    [in] SOCKET s
);  
    - Returns an integer- **WSACleanup**    - Cleans up the WinSock library resources. Takes no arguments and returns an integer    - ```c=      int WSAAPI WSACleanup();





For any of the functions above that return an integer, a return value of 0 means the call succeeded, otherwise it is an error code.

PID 0 An Idle Process used to swap all pages of processs to&#x2F;from memory&#x2F;backing store. “Swap Process” It was there, so Linux always have CPU to execute something If you try to execute ps command on the clean Ubuntu Linux, you’ll find there was no process with “PID0” Idle systemcall not supported since kernal 2.3.13
Then how can we prove that PID0 really exist?
Demo Using bpftrace for tracking the kernel function hrtimer_wakeup hrtimer_wakeup mean to: Waking up processes Move them into runnable process
hrtimer_wakeup source code ##Refer to https://elixir.bootlin.com/linux/latest/source/kernel/time/hrtimer.c#L2000static enum hrtimer_restart hrtimer_wakeup(struct hrtimer *timer)&#123; struct hrtimer_sleeper *t = container_of(timer, struct hrtimer_sleeper, timer); struct task_struct *task = t-&gt;task; t-&gt;task = NULL; if (task) wake_up_process(task); return HRTIMER_NORESTART;&#125;
I open a new Amazon EC2 Instance with Ubuntu 22 LTS AMI for testing. Command sudo bpftrace -e &#x27;kfunc:hrtimer_wakeup &#123;printf(&quot;%s:%d\n&quot;,curtask-&gt;comm,curtask-&gt;pid); &#125;&#x27;
It shows there are only 1 instance of swapper: swapper/0 with PID0. If you open a new VM with 3 to 4 vCPU, then you execute the command again, You will find that there are 3 or4 swapper instances with PID0, so there may have swapper/0, swapper/1, swapper/2 appears. PPID If you try to execute the command ps -eaf on your local system, you may find there are a column named PPID, It means the Parrent Process ID. In Linux systems, A PPID is always assigned to every process ID. It tells us which process started a particular process. Therefore, the PPID value of 0 for the init process indicates that the init process has no parent. Reference[1] Refer to “The Linux Process Journey” by Dr. Shlommi Bountnaru[2] https://www.baeldung.com/linux/process-pid-0

